 replace('legal-review',title='Accountability for executive decisions',practiceType='Extended-response skills practice — not a complete IA1 item',
  stimulus='Original fictional Queensland governance sources for practice.\n\nSource 1 — Agency record: A Queensland government agency made 400 decisions in a year. Ninety people asked for reasons. Seventy of these requests received no written response. The agency has an internal review process.\n\nSource 2 — Community legal service statement: “People need to understand a decision before they can challenge it. Review should be accessible and independent of the original decision-maker.”\n\nSource 3 — Agency administration statement: “More reporting and external reviews would require extra staff and funding. The agency must also resolve applications promptly.”',
  question='Analyse the issue of accountability for decisions made by the Queensland government agency in Sources 1–3. Examine two viewpoints and their consequences. Evaluate two legal alternatives and justify a recommendation using legal criteria. Discuss one implication of your recommendation.',
  answer=['The issue is accountability for executive decisions: people affected by the agency’s decisions may be unable to obtain reasons or effectively challenge them. Source 1 identifies the scale of unanswered requests and the existing internal review mechanism.',
  'The community legal service prioritises reasons and independent review. This could improve procedural fairness and people’s ability to challenge errors. The agency administration prioritises resources and timely decisions. Extra reporting and review requirements could increase costs and delay other applications.',
  'One legal alternative is a statutory duty to give written reasons, with defined exceptions and response deadlines, while retaining internal review. A second is that duty combined with accessible independent review. The first improves transparency at lower administrative cost, but internal review may leave concerns about independence. The second provides a stronger accountability safeguard but requires additional resources.',
  'A defensible recommendation is reasons plus accessible independent review, justified by procedural fairness and accountability. Fund the review function and set reasonable time limits to address the agency’s resourcing concerns. An implication is additional expenditure and demand for trained reviewers. A different recommendation can earn credit if supported by the sources and legal criteria.'],
  points=[{'text':t,'marks':m} for t,m in [('Analyses the nature and scope of the identified governance issue using the sources',2),('Examines two viewpoints and their consequences',4),('Evaluates two legal alternatives and justifies a recommendation using legal criteria',3),('Discusses a relevant implication',1)]],marks=10)
